## Structural traits every interface may pick up for free with 'implement', overriding only the
## methods whose default isn't good enough.
##
## Every default below is compiler-implemented: the body written here never actually compiles (see
## LuauGenerator.GetOrCreateTraitDefault's '_internalTraitDefaults' table) because a real
## implementation has to walk a value's fields generically, and nothing short of a Luau runtime
## helper can do that - one which stays internal to the compiler rather than becoming a callable
## Loom name. What's written is a correctly-typed placeholder only, so the trait still has something
## to type-check an 'implement' block's override against.

trait Display {
    ### A human-readable representation of this value: every field, recursively, rendered the way
    ### Rust's derived 'Debug' would. Override this where a more specific representation is wanted
    ### instead.
    fn to_string(): string -> "";
}

trait Eq {
    ### Whether this value and 'other' are the same: recursive, field-by-field structural equality,
    ### not reference equality - two separately constructed values with identical fields are equal.
    ### Override this where a shallower or field-specific comparison is wanted instead.
    fn equals(other: unknown): bool -> false;
}

trait Hash {
    ### A deterministic structural hash: two values 'Eq.equals' calls equal always hash the same.
    ### Two unequal values hashing the same costs nothing but a wasted comparison downstream, so this
    ### only needs to be consistent with 'equals', never collision-free. Override this alongside
    ### 'Eq.equals' whenever that is overridden, or the two can disagree about which fields matter.
    fn hash(): number -> 0;
}
